                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "The game still holds up after 20 years.\n\nAlthough it was Wadjet Eye Games’ first commercial game (and therefore had some game-design mistakes), it clearly laid the groundwork for its supernatural and mysterious world and kickstarted a brand-new IP for the company.\n\n[b][u]Notebook Mechanic[/u][/b]\n\nThe game’s notebook mechanic is fun to use. Collecting clues as inventory items was something of a novel idea at the time (apparently used previously in Discworld Noir in 1999).\n\nForming new ideas by combining seemingly unrelated clues enhances the detective experience. However, it can also block your progress: even if you as the player have made an obvious connection, the character you control won’t recognize it until you explicitly link the clues in the notebook. This can leave you locked out of new dialogue options without realizing why.",
